Generic Coding Rules for Flutter/Dart Projects
These coding standards ensure consistency, scalability, performance, and maintainability for Flutter/Dart projects across web, mobile, or desktop applications.

1. Dart Code Style
Objective: Ensure consistent, readable, and maintainable code.

Rules:

Run dart format . for uniform formatting.
Use flutter_lints in analysis_options.yaml with rules:
always_declare_return_types
prefer_const_constructors
avoid_dynamic_calls (except for justified interop)
prefer_final_fields for immutability
avoid_empty_else for clarity
use_function_type_syntax_for_parameters
unnecessary_null_comparison for null safety


Follow naming conventions:
PascalCase for classes (e.g., DataProcessor)
camelCase for variables/functions (e.g., processData)
kCamelCase for constants (e.g., kDefaultTimeout)


Ensure null safety compliance in all code.


Example:
const kDefaultTimeout = 30;
final String processData(String input) => input.toUpperCase();

class DataProcessor {
  final String id;
  const DataProcessor({required this.id});
}


Commands:
dart format .
flutter analyze
dart fix --apply




2. File Structure
Objective: Promote modularity and ease of navigation.

Rules:

Organize code by feature or layer (e.g., lib/screens/, lib/services/).
Limit files to a single responsibility (e.g., one class or function).
Use a layered architecture: UI (screens/, widgets/), logic (services/), and data (models/).


Example Structure:
lib/
├── config/
│   └── app_config.dart
├── models/
│   └── data_model.dart
├── screens/
│   └── main_screen.dart
├── services/
│   └── network_service.dart
├── utils/
│   └── helpers.dart
├── widgets/
│   └── reusable_widget.dart
└── theme/
    └── app_theme.dart




3. State Management
Objective: Handle state efficiently and scalably.

Rules:

Use provider for most projects due to simplicity and performance.
Evaluate alternatives (riverpod, bloc, Redux) for complex apps (e.g., large state trees, async dependencies).
Avoid setState in large or complex widgets to prevent performance issues.
Use dependency injection (provider, get_it) for testable services.


Example:
class DataProvider with ChangeNotifier {
  String _data = '';
  String get data => _data;
  void updateData(String newData) {
    _data = newData;
    notifyListeners();
  }
}


Dependencies:
dependencies:
  provider: ^6.0.0
  get_it: ^7.6.0




4. Widget Development
Objective: Build modular, efficient, and responsive widgets.

Rules:

Decompose large widgets into smaller, reusable components.
Use const constructors to optimize rendering and tree shaking.
Assign Keys (e.g., ValueKey) for testing and state preservation.
Ensure responsiveness with LayoutBuilder, MediaQuery, Expanded, or Flexible.
Avoid unconstrained widgets (e.g., ListView without SizedBox parent).
Enhance accessibility with Semantics.
Debug layout issues using debugPaintSizeEnabled or Flutter DevTools.


Example:
class ReusableWidget extends StatelessWidget {
  const ReusableWidget({super.key});
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return LayoutBuilder(
      builder: (context, constraints) => ConstrainedBox(
        constraints: BoxConstraints(maxWidth: constraints.maxWidth * 0.9),
        child: ListView.builder(
          itemCount: items.length,
          itemBuilder: (context, index) => ListTile(title: Text('Item $index')),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}




5. API and Service Integration
Objective: Integrate external services reliably and securely.

Rules:

Handle sensitive operations server-side where applicable.
Implement robust error handling and response validation.
Use dependency injection for services to enhance testability.


Example:
class NetworkService {
  Future<String> fetchData() async {
    try {
      final response = await http.get(Uri.parse('https://api.example.com/data'));
      if (response.statusCode == 200) {
        return response.body;
      } else {
      throw Exception('Request failed');
    }
    } catch (e) {
      throw Exception('Network error: $e');
    }
    }
  }
}




6. Performance Optimization
Objective: Ensure fast and efficient app performance.

Rules:

Minimize bundle size with tree shaking` and deferred loading.
Use ListView.builder for large lists to reduce rendering overhead.
Optimize images with compression or packages like cached_network_image.
Minimize overdraw with clipBehavior on widgets.
Profile performance using Flutter DevTools to identify bottlenecks.
For web apps, choose between html renderer (lightweight) or canvaskit (rich graphics) based on needs.
Performance goals:
Bundle size: < 2MB for initial load
First Contentful Paint: < 2 seconds




Build Commands:
flutter build web --release --tree-shake-icons --web-renderer html
flutter build web --analyze-size


Dependencies:
dependencies:
  cached_network_image: ^3.2.0




7. Testing
Objective: Guarantee reliability through comprehensive testing.

Rules:

Write unit tests for logic, widget tests for UI, and integration tests for key flows.
Use mockito for mocking dependencies.
Target >80% code coverage.
Test critical error scenarios (e.g., network failures, invalid input).


Example Widget Test:
testWidgets('Widget renders correctly', (WidgetTester tester) async {
  await tester.pumpWidget(const MyApp());
  expect(find.byKey(const Key('content_widget')), findsOneWidget);
});


Dependencies:
dev_dependencies:
  mockito: ^5.4.2
  flutter_test:
    sdk: flutter




8. Security
Objective: Protect sensitive data and ensure robust validation.

Rules:

Store sensitive credentials in environment variables or secure storage.
Validate inputs on client and server sides.
Use HTTPS for all network communications.
Regularly review access controls for production environments.


Example Validation:
String? validateInput(String? value) {
  if (value == null || value.isEmpty) {
    return 'This field is required';
  }
  return null;
}




9. Documentation
Objective: Keep code and project well-documented.

Rules:

Add inline comments for complex logic.
Generate API docs with flutter doc.
Maintain an up-to-date README.md with setup and usage instructions.


Example:
/// Processes input data and returns a result.
String processInput(String input) {
  // Implementation
  return input.toUpperCase();
}


Commands:
dart doc




10. Deployment & Quality Assurance
Objective: Ensure smooth and reliable deployments.

Pre-deployment Checklist:
flutter analyze                    # No errors
dart format --set-exit-if-changed  # No changes needed
flutter test                      # All tests pass
 flutter build web --release        # Builds successfully


Monitoring:
npm install -g @lhci-cli
lhci autorun --upload.target=temporary-public-storage




Conclusion
These generic rules provide a flexible, high-quality foundation for any Flutter/Dart project. They are designed to ensure consistency, performance, and reliability while allowing adaptation to specific project needs.
